T={}
def kit(name,spec,why,query='',decision='confirm'):
 return dict(name=name,specification=spec,why=why,query=query,decision=decision)
def make(pid,title,summary,fish,season,ev,sections,kits,mistakes,questions,**extra):
 d={'title':title,'summary':summary,'fish':fish,'season':season,'seasonWindows':[],
 'sourceIds':list(dict.fromkeys(s for s,c in ev)), 'evidence':[{'source':s,'claim':c} for s,c in ev],
 'sections':[{'title':h,'text':p} for h,p in sections], 'kit':kits,'mistakes':mistakes,'questions':questions,
 'availabilityNote':'Reference resources are not confirmed Atlas inventory. No operator relationship, price or date availability is asserted.'}
 d.update(extra);T[pid]=d
make('US-NC-001','Cape Hatteras surf fishing: beach access, setups and a lighter packing list',
'Choose a beach outing rather than mixing surf, pier and offshore advice. Plan access first, then match your presentation and what you can carry.',
[], 'The National Park Service describes spring and fall as broad fishing highlights and distinguishes summer offshore opportunity. That does not establish a species-specific surf calendar. Check a local beach report, tide and access conditions before committing your dates.',
[('release-caha','Cape Hatteras National Seashore lists surf, pier, sound and offshore options. It identifies Oregon Inlet Fishing Center separately from beach fishing; those are different outings, not interchangeable access points.')],
[('Make the beach the unit of the plan','Pick the beach sector and the route from your accommodation before choosing a large cooler or a cart. Write down whether your party will walk, use an authorized vehicle route, or switch to a pier. A beach outing should not inherit an offshore charter’s equipment list. A pier alternative also needs its own access confirmation rather than an assumption that all tackle transfers unchanged.'),
('Build around a presentation','Choose between casting lures and holding a bait presentation before selecting rod length, lure or sinker range, reel capacity and leader. Ask a local tackle shop to check the complete combination for the intended target and conditions. A rod selected only because its title says “surf” may not suit the actual casting weight. Keep a modest set of terminal spares rather than buying every local lure category.'),
('Carry only what the outing needs','For a walking trip, separate the kit used at the water from spare clothes and a larger transport cooler left at the vehicle. Put water, personal essentials and a weather layer ahead of duplicate rods in the carrying plan. Existing suitable sunglasses, clothing and a dry bag should stay marked as owned. Choose footwear from the actual access arrangement rather than automatically adding chest waders.'),
('Build an alternative into the day','Decide in advance when a beach outing will be postponed or changed to a different format. Confirm the alternative itself is available; it is not enough to write “go to a pier” in an itinerary. Save the local information source with the plan and revisit it close to departure. No current target species, safe surf condition or open vehicle route is asserted here.')],
[kit('Presentation-matched surf setup','Have rod casting rating, chosen terminal weight, reel, line and leader checked together.','Purchase only after deciding bait versus lure and the local target.'),
 kit('Compact terminal and handling kit','Choose locally suitable hooks or lures, leader material, pliers and a compact tackle container.','The presentation determines the components; do not buy a generic all-species assortment.'),
 kit('Carry system for your access','Use a shoulder pack, suitable existing bag or cart only where the chosen route supports it.','Walking distance changes what belongs at the beach.','fishing shoulder tackle bag', 'optional'),
 kit('Weather and eye protection','Reuse suitable polarized eyewear and layers; choose footwear for the actual beach access.','A beach packing list is not a reason to purchase offshore equipment.','polarized fishing sunglasses','optional')],
['Combining beach, pier and offshore advice into one equipment purchase.','Buying a cart or waders before confirming the actual access.'],
['Which beach sector and access route suit our planned outing?','What presentation and casting range fit the target and conditions?','What alternative should we arrange when the beach plan does not work?'],water='Saltwater',access=['Bank'])
make('US-NC-002','Oregon Inlet offshore fishing: choose the charter before buying tackle',
'Compare the full offshore day with shorter coastal trips, then prepare for the booked boat instead of purchasing a second boat’s worth of equipment.',
['Yellowfin tuna','Bluefin tuna','Mahi-mahi'],
'The fleet describes several offshore targets but that is not a promise they overlap on your dates. The park’s broad summer-offshore description is not a month-by-month tuna calendar. Ask the selected captain which target and trip duration are realistic for your visit.',
[('release-oregon-inlet','The fishing center separates half-day inshore options from full-day offshore trips and names tuna and mahi-mahi among offshore targets. Its FAQ says its fleet supplies tackle and bait; fish cleaning is a separate paid service.'),('release-caha','The National Park Service identifies Oregon Inlet Fishing Center as the seashore’s charter-fishing resource, distinct from beaches and piers.')],
[('Choose the trip duration and target together','When comparing quotes, ask for the departure, expected return and the difference between total trip duration and fishing time. Name your preferred target but also state the group’s experience and comfort with a full boat day. An offshore itinerary should not be sold to the rest of the party as the same commitment as a short inlet trip. Confirm the boat and date rather than treating a fleet description as available inventory.'),
('Confirm what the booked boat includes','Use the fleet’s supplied-equipment statement as a starting point and confirm it for your actual reservation. Ask whether a personal rod is welcome and whether it is useful for that day’s method. Do not buy large reels, line or trolling gear solely to complete a generic offshore list. A short written list of inclusions can remove the most expensive unnecessary purchases.'),
('Separate onboard needs from the ride home','The operator recommends sun protection, weather-appropriate clothing, non-slip shoes and refreshments, with a small onboard cooler and a larger transport cooler at the vehicle. Ask about deck storage and any cooler-size preferences before packing. Keep the fish-cleaning arrangement separate from the charter quote and decide how your party would transport a retained catch without assuming there will be one.'),
('Use the plan as a captain briefing','Record the group size, experience, preferred target and equipment questions in the inquiry. Add an alternative date where your itinerary permits, and ask how the operator communicates changes. This guide does not contain live boat availability. The inquiry button creates a preparation message, not a paid reservation or a claim that Atlas represents the fleet.')],
[kit('Boat-supplied tackle confirmation','Confirm rods, reels, terminal tackle and bait with the booked boat.','Do not buy offshore tackle the crew will provide.'),kit('Deck footwear','Use comfortable, non-slip footwear accepted by the operator.','Choose for a boat day rather than wading.','non slip fishing deck shoes','consider'),kit('Personal sun and weather kit','Suitable sunglasses, hat, layers and a compact weather-protected bag.','Reuse owned gear and choose layers close to departure.','waterproof fishing dry bag','optional'),kit('Onboard versus transport storage','Confirm cooler space and fish-cleaning/transport arrangements.','A large cooler may belong in the vehicle, not on the deck.')],
['Treating an offshore trip as interchangeable with a short inshore outing.','Buying technical tackle before checking inclusions.'],['Which target and departure length suit our dates?','What exactly is supplied by our boat?','How do you handle changes and fish cleaning?'])
# Two genuinely different local decisions, supported by scoped agency evidence.
make('US-SC-001','Charleston inshore redfish: choose the habitat and fish size before the kit',
'Build a marsh or harbor plan around the intended fish and access, rather than treating every redfish outing as the same technique.',
['Red drum'], 'South Carolina DNR distinguishes juvenile estuary use from adult open-water behavior. Its 2024 article describes late-summer spawning aggregations and encourages considering post-spawn opportunities; an aggregation is not an automatic recommendation for a trip. This guide does not rank Charleston months.',
[('release-sc-drum','SCDNR describes red drum across tidal-creek, oyster, beach and nearshore habitats, with different juvenile and adult habitat use.'),('release-sc-big-drum','The agency’s August 2024 article identifies Charleston Harbor among adult aggregation areas and discusses the implications of targeting large spawning fish.')],
[('Decide what “redfish trip” means','Tell the guide whether you want to learn light-tackle marsh presentations or pursue larger fish in a different setting. Ask which plan suits the experience level and handling ability of the group. Choose the relevant creek, harbor or other area with local help. Charleston is the departure region, not a single uniform fishing spot.'),('Let the tide inform the itinerary','Request the meeting time and expected access window for the chosen area rather than imposing a generic morning start. Ask which changes would trigger a different route. The guide should explain how the intended presentation fits the actual water being fished. This is a planning question, not a claim that one tide produces the best fishing everywhere.'),('Buy the terminal system last','Settle artificial versus natural-bait presentation, expected fish size and the crew’s handling approach before matching line, leader and hooks. A stronger-sounding rod is not automatically a better purchase. Check whether an owned setup is suitable and whether the boat has an appropriate landing tool. Keep specialized terminal purchases unresolved until that local decision is made.'),('Prepare for the chosen access','A boat-based marsh outing does not automatically require waders. Pack footwear the operator accepts, weather layers, sunglasses and compact personal storage. For an independent shore option, confirm actual public access before buying a different kit. Ask how fish will be handled and when the outing should move or stop rather than treating a photograph as the main objective.')],
[kit('Target-specific rod and line','Match expected fish size, presentation and local structure with the guide.','A juvenile marsh scenario and adult open-water scenario need not use the same kit.'),kit('Leader and terminal components','Choose only after the bait or lure method is agreed.','Compatible parts matter more than an oversized assortment.'),kit('Footwear for the actual outing','Use boat footwear unless wading is explicitly part of the plan.','Do not automatically purchase chest waders.','fishing deck shoes','optional'),kit('Handling equipment check','Ask whether the crew supplies an appropriate landing tool and release equipment.','Avoid duplicating boat equipment and agree on handling before the first fish.')],['Using spawning-aggregation information as a blanket “best time” recommendation.','Assuming harbor and shallow-marsh tackle are identical.'],['Which habitat and size class are we targeting?','How does the planned access relate to the tide?','What do you supply for landing and release?'])
make('US-SC-002','Beaufort and Port Royal Sound redfish: boat choice, access and preparation',
'Separate a shallow-marsh outing from a larger-sound program, then choose equipment and a meeting plan for that specific experience.',
['Red drum'], 'The agency’s Port Royal Sound discussion concerns adult fish and late-summer spawning context. It is not a universal seasonal calendar for all nearby marsh fishing. Ask a local provider for a target- and habitat-specific plan for your actual dates; keep spotted seatrout unconfirmed in this edition.',
[('release-sc-big-drum','SCDNR includes Port Royal Sound among areas used by large red drum and recommends careful handling and attention to spawning context.'),('release-sc-drum','The species profile supports distinguishing shallow estuary habitat from adult open-water use, rather than treating a sound and every adjoining creek as one fishing scenario.')],
[('Choose the departure and water together','Ask whether the planned boat will work in sheltered creeks, a particular marsh area or a more open part of the sound. Confirm the actual meeting point instead of navigating simply to Beaufort. A quote should describe the trip format and duration; a town name alone does not tell the party what sort of day it is purchasing.'),('Make the alternative local','Agree on an alternative route or date with the provider. Do not automatically substitute a Charleston outing, because travel, departure and habitat assumptions would change. Put those choices into the plan so that everyone knows which bookings and gear decisions must be revisited if the primary outing changes.'),('Choose presentation, then tackle','Ask the guide to assess an owned setup against the selected method and intended fish before buying another rod. Confirm whether the crew supplies terminal tackle and landing equipment. Natural-bait and artificial presentations should have separate component choices, even when both appear under a broad redfish label. No exact line strength is prescribed without that context.'),('Keep the personal kit simple','Use a small day bag, suitable boat footwear and adjustable sun or weather protection for a confirmed boat trip. Leave wading equipment out unless wading is part of the agreement. Decide which group member carries shared essentials instead of duplicating them. The preparation message should ask about boat storage, timing and inclusions, not merely whether fishing is “good.”')],
[kit('Method-matched tackle','Confirm bait versus artificial, fish size and the guide’s preferred system.','Avoid transferring a generic flats setup to every sound outing.'),kit('Onboard personal storage','Choose compact storage after the boat’s available space is known.','A small shared kit may replace several bulky bags.','small roll top dry bag','optional'),kit('Weather and footwear system','Prepare for the specific boat and route; no automatic waders.','Access determines personal equipment.','non slip boat shoes','optional'),kit('Landing and release provision','Confirm the operator’s appropriate net or other handling equipment.','This is a provision question before it is a purchase.')],['Using a different town’s meeting or tide assumptions.','Treating a candidate second species as verified by a red-drum source.'],['Which water and meeting point are included?','What is our local alternative if the route changes?','Can you assess my existing tackle before I buy more?'])
make('US-GA-001','Lake Lanier fishing: choose striped bass or a black-bass plan first',
'Use the target and launch area to choose between a guided open-water program, a bank outing and a smaller upper-lake plan.',
['Striped bass','Largemouth bass'], 'Don Carter’s official page gives upper-lake spring and summer/fall habitat context. That is not a lake-wide monthly ranking. Confirm the chosen target, lake sector and current presentation; this guide avoids treating differing spotted/Alabama-bass labels across agency pages as interchangeable taxonomy.',
[('release-lanier','Don Carter State Park describes upper Lake Lanier fishing, shoreline and paddling options, and several bass targets.'),('release-ga-forecast','The agency’s Lake Lanier forecast listing includes striped bass and largemouth bass and uses Alabama-bass terminology for another black-bass target.')],
[('Choose the fish before selecting a package','A customer saying “bass fishing” may be asking for very different outings. Specify striped bass or a particular black-bass program before comparing boats, lure selections and trip duration. Where the local species naming is unclear, ask the provider to identify the target rather than silently filling a planner field with a familiar label.'),('Use a launch-sector plan','The official Don Carter information is useful for an upper-lake visit, but it should not become directions for every part of Lanier. Choose the meeting area and verify how bank, paddle or guided-boat access will work there. Decide whether the group can manage its own transport and launch logistics before renting equipment. A geographic pin is not confirmation of a usable access route.'),('Match equipment to the selected method','Request the proposed presentation and the supplied-equipment list from a guide. A bank-casting kit should not automatically inherit a guided trolling program’s rod and storage requirements. For personal tackle, match the actual lure or bait approach and expected load with an appropriately balanced rod, reel and line. Save expensive purchases until those specifications are known.'),('Make the day bag fit the platform','For an independent outing, pack only what can be managed at the chosen access. For a boat booking, confirm storage and provided equipment. Keep hydration, personal weather protection and a way to protect essentials on the list while removing duplicate rods and large tackle boxes the plan does not use. Check current conditions through the agency resources rather than treating this article as a live report.')],
[kit('Target-specific tackle','Choose striped bass versus a confirmed black-bass target and the actual method.','The word bass alone does not define a setup.'),kit('Local presentation components','Ask which lure or bait approach applies to the selected sector.','Avoid buying unrelated trolling and shore-casting kits.'),kit('Personal flotation provision','For a paddle or boat outing, confirm properly fitting flotation and what is supplied.','A booking description is not an equipment-fit check.'),kit('Compact weather-protected storage','Reuse suitable storage sized to the bank, kayak or boat plan.','Keep the carry load appropriate to the platform.','small waterproof fishing bag','optional')],['Treating upper-lake advice as instructions for the entire reservoir.','Choosing gear before clarifying which bass and presentation are intended.'],['What exact target and method will we use?','Which launch or bank area is the plan built around?','What flotation and tackle do you provide?'],access=['Bank','Guided boat'])
make('US-GA-002','Chattahoochee trout below Buford Dam: pick the reach and check releases',
'Build a reach-specific bank or guided plan. Flow information and actual access come before the decision to wade or buy equipment.',
['Rainbow trout','Brown trout'], 'The park identifies a designated trout-water corridor below Buford Dam, while release timing and conditions must be checked for the actual outing. Do not turn that corridor designation into a promise of safe wading or a uniform seasonal pattern. No real-time discharge is embedded in this guide.',
[('release-chat-fishing','The National Park Service describes the Buford Dam–Peachtree Creek corridor as designated trout water.'),('release-chat-conditions','The park directs visitors to water-release and river-condition information, including releases from Buford and Morgan Falls dams.'),('release-ga-forecast','Georgia DNR lists rainbow and brown trout among Chattahoochee forecast targets.')],
[('Name the reach and access mode','“Below Buford Dam” is a starting point, not a complete itinerary. Choose a particular reach and a verified public access or guide meeting point. Say explicitly whether the outing is bank-based or boat-based. This edition does not offer independent wading as a default simply because the species are trout.'),('Make the flow check part of departure','Save the official release and condition resources beside the meeting instructions. Recheck them close to the outing and follow the local provider’s decision about changing or canceling the route. A timetable is not a personal assurance that a reach will remain suitable, and an earlier observation does not establish current flow. When uncertain, keep the outing out of the water rather than improvising a crossing.'),('Choose the presentation for the selected reach','Ask whether the plan is fly or conventional fishing and which tackle the provider supplies. Match an owned rod and reel to the chosen presentation, then select leader, line and terminal components. Do not purchase a large assortment of flies and spinning lures before that decision. A few locally selected options are more useful than an unfocused kit.'),('Keep preparation tied to the route','Pack appropriate footwear for the confirmed bank or boat arrangement, personal layers and compact protected storage. Do not assume the presence of a river means chest waders are needed. Put the return plan and alternative meeting arrangements in the saved itinerary so that a route change does not turn into an improvised access search.')],
[kit('Reach-specific fly or spinning system','Confirm presentation and supplied tackle for the chosen reach.','Match the setup only after the local plan is known.'),kit('Bank or boat footwear','Choose for confirmed access; wading is not assumed.','Avoid purchasing waders before a local assessment.'),kit('Flow and access information','Keep the official release and condition links available before departure.','This is a planning requirement, not a product purchase.'),kit('Protected essentials storage','Use an owned suitable bag or compact dry storage.','Carry only the personal items needed for the selected reach.','small waterproof roll top bag','optional')],['Assuming designated trout water implies safe independent wading.','Using yesterday’s flow information as today’s access confirmation.'],['Which reach and access point are we using?','Who checks releases and communicates changes?','Is my existing tackle suitable for the planned presentation?'],access=['Bank','Guided boat'])
make('US-FL-001','Islamorada flats fishing: bonefish, permit and the right personal kit',
'Choose the target and fly or conventional approach before buying equipment. A skiff day is not automatically a wading trip.',
['Bonefish','Permit'], 'The tourism authority’s April 2023 regional guide gives different seasonal context for bonefish and permit. Use it as background, not a current Islamorada forecast. Ask a local captain how target, temperature, tide, wind and visibility shape the options for your dates; do not reuse offshore seasonality for a flats trip.',
[('release-keys-islamorada','The destination authority separates Islamorada’s shallow-water fishing from offshore experiences.'),('release-keys-season','The authority’s 2023 Keys-wide guide names bonefish and permit among shallow-water targets and describes different seasonal patterns for individual species.')],
[('Choose a primary target','Tell the guide whether bonefish or permit is the main objective, while leaving room for an alternative the captain considers realistic. A broad “flats grand slam” label should not be mistaken for a promise to catch multiple species. Ask how much instruction and casting practice the day includes and describe the group’s actual experience.'),('Choose fly or conventional before shopping','Have the provider specify the method and confirm what it supplies. For owned gear, check line, leader, reel capacity and the intended presentation together. Avoid selecting a rod solely because a product listing says saltwater. Fly and conventional approaches should remain separate equipment choices rather than items combined in one recommended bundle.'),('Keep skiff and wading preparation separate','This plan defaults to a guided skiff. Ask about deck footwear and line management. Do not add chest waders or dedicated wading boots unless the actual itinerary includes locally suitable wading. Use existing sun-protective clothing, polarized eyewear and compact storage where appropriate. Select layer weight close to travel using the real conditions, not a tropical label alone.'),('Make the booking inquiry specific','Include the preferred target, dates, number of anglers and experience. Ask for the departure location, total time, gear provision and what would trigger a different plan. Treat the source resources as research and operator-discovery aids. No listed date, price or partnership has been confirmed by Atlas in this edition.')],
[kit('Target and method-matched system','Confirm bonefish versus permit and fly versus conventional with the guide.','Choose the compatible rod, reel, line and leader only after that decision.'),kit('Polarized eyewear','Reuse suitable polarized glasses with a secure fit.','A useful personal item, not a reason to replace good owned equipment.','polarized fishing sunglasses','optional'),kit('Skiff clothing and footwear','Confirm preferred deck footwear and sun/weather layers.','No automatic waders for a skiff booking.'),kit('Flies, lures or bait provision','Ask which presentations are supplied and which exact extras are requested.','Do not buy an all-destination saltwater assortment.')],['Applying offshore tackle or calendar advice to a flats trip.','Buying waders for a boat-only itinerary.'],['Which target fits my experience and dates?','What fly or conventional equipment do you provide?','Does this trip actually involve wading?'],access=['Skiff'])
make('US-FL-002','Lake Okeechobee bass fishing: choose the sector, cover and access first',
'Use water level, vegetation and the actual departure area to narrow a vast lake into a workable bass-fishing plan.',
['Largemouth bass'], 'FWC links fishing distribution to water level and vegetation. The forecast page includes a dated June 2026 summer observation, not a current September water-level feed. Check the latest local and agency information before selecting a launch or buying presentation-specific equipment.',
[('release-okee-habitat','FWC describes Okeechobee as a shallow lake with extensive marsh habitat supporting largemouth bass and black crappie.'),('release-okee-fishing','FWC’s fishing guide connects changing water level and vegetation with fish distribution and distinguishes presentations around edges from those in heavier cover.')],
[('Start with one sector','Ask which launch area and portion of the lake the planned day uses. Do not assume lodging on one shore is convenient for a departure on another. Resolve the transport and meeting point before deciding the duration. A useful itinerary names the area and an alternative rather than promising to cover the entire lake.'),('Choose the cover and presentation','Ask whether the day is built around casting near edges, fishing heavier cover or another confirmed approach. That decision changes the appropriate rod, line and lure system. Do not automatically place a heavy-cover setup and a lighter casting setup on the same shopping list when the trip will use only one. If a guide supplies equipment, record that before making any technical purchase.'),('Read reports with their dates attached','Keep the observation date with any water-level or fishing note. A summer example can explain a method without proving it is the best choice for your visit. Save the agency forecast and ask the local provider which parts apply to the selected sector now. When a launch or vegetation pattern changes, update the plan instead of simply changing the date in the article.'),('Pack for the booked platform','For a guided boat outing, use compact personal storage, suitable footwear and forecast-based layers. Ask whether drinks, bait and equipment are included and whether there are storage restrictions. A bank or independent boat outing needs its own verified access arrangement. The primary way to reduce wasted purchases is to settle those platform decisions first.')],
[kit('Cover-specific rod, reel and line','Match the actual edge, open-water or heavy-cover presentation.','Choose components for the plan, not every possible bass technique.'),kit('Locally selected presentation kit','Confirm artificial versus bait and whether supplies are included.','A single outing does not require a tackle-store assortment.'),kit('Boat-day personal protection','Reuse suitable hat, eyewear, layers and footwear.','Select for the actual duration and weather.','fishing sun hat','optional'),kit('Launch and meeting-point check','Confirm the chosen sector, departure and access close to the trip.','Do not turn a dated water-level note into current access advice.')],['Choosing lodging and a launch without checking travel between them.','Presenting a dated forecast as current lake conditions.'],['Which sector and launch are we using?','What cover and presentation will we focus on?','Which equipment and supplies are included?'],access=['Guided boat','Bank'])
make('US-TX-002','Lake Fork bass fishing: cover, launch choice and a compatible setup',
'Match your bass presentation to cover and access. Keep navigation planning separate from the lure shopping list.',
['Largemouth bass'], 'TPWD describes mid-February through April as a notable spring largemouth period, with different opportunities and presentations in other seasons. This is background rather than a guarantee or a live bite report. Ask how the current conditions affect your chosen sector and method.',
[('release-fork','TPWD identifies largemouth bass as a principal Lake Fork target and describes timber, vegetation and constructed cover. It also warns about submerged obstructions.'),('release-fork-access','TPWD lists public and private access choices, including Sabine River Authority ramps. Named facilities still require a trip-date availability check.')],
[('Choose the launch with the guide or route','Pick the area you intend to fish before selecting a convenient-looking launch on a map. Confirm the meeting point, parking and return arrangement. For an independent boat outing, address local navigation and current access before discussing lure colors. A buoyed route is not a guarantee that every nearby area is clear of obstructions.'),('Match cover to the setup','Ask whether the plan focuses on vegetation, timber, docks or a different verified feature. Choose the rod’s working range, line and terminal components for the actual presentation rather than combining every bass method in one kit. Check owned equipment with the guide before buying a specialized outfit. One coherent system is more useful than a premium reel paired with unsuitable line or lure weight.'),('Use seasonal background without promising a trophy','The agency’s spring guidance helps frame an initial conversation, but it does not establish a personal catch outcome. Keep the chosen method and current observations beside their dates. Decide whether the trip is primarily instructional, exploratory or focused on a specific technique, so that the value of the outing does not depend on a single fish.'),('Plan the day, not just the tackle box','Separate boat-provided equipment from personal items. Bring appropriate eyewear and clothing, refreshments as agreed, and only the spare tackle requested for the method. Keep larger storage at the vehicle when the operator prefers a compact onboard load. Record an alternative for a route or weather change, and do not treat any source link here as a confirmed reservation.')],
[kit('Cover-matched bass system','Confirm presentation, rod range, line and terminal components as a set.','A Lake Fork label on a product is not a fit check.'),kit('Small, focused presentation selection','Select local options for the method instead of a universal tackle bundle.','Buy only the missing components after guide confirmation.'),kit('Navigation and launch preparation','Verify the actual ramp and route; address submerged obstacles.','This is a preparation requirement before equipment shopping.'),kit('Personal eyewear and storage','Reuse suitable polarized eyewear and a compact weather-protected bag.','Keep the onboard load consistent with the trip.','compact waterproof fishing bag','optional')],['Assuming marked lanes remove every navigation concern.','Treating a notable spring period as a catch guarantee.'],['Which launch and cover will the trip use?','Will my rod and line suit your presentation?','What should stay in the vehicle instead of coming aboard?'],access=['Guided boat','Bank'])
make('US-TX-001','Port Aransas bay fishing: separate skiff, kayak and shore preparation',
'Choose a bay outing and the actual access mode before building a redfish or seatrout shopping list.',
['Red drum','Spotted seatrout'], 'The local tourism authority describes broad year-round opportunities and seasonal preferences. Those labels do not establish a particular day’s access or fish activity. Select the target, tide-dependent route and weather alternative with a local source before setting a detailed itinerary.',
[('release-porta','Port Aransas tourism lists redfish and spotted seatrout among bay targets and distinguishes guided boating, wading and kayak options around the local bays and flats.')],
[('Choose the platform first','A guided skiff, a kayak and an independent shore outing solve different access problems. Choose one before adding footwear, storage, flotation or a second rod to the plan. Ask a provider to specify the actual departure and what water the trip will cover. A bay-fishing page is not confirmation that a particular shoreline can be reached from the road.'),('Choose a target and presentation','Tell the local shop or guide whether redfish or seatrout is the intended focus and ask which approach fits the outing. Check an owned setup before replacing it. Natural-bait and artificial presentations may call for different components; do not buy both entire systems unless the planned day genuinely needs both. The guide contains no exact locally vetted product model.'),('Separate owned, rented and supplied items','For a guide booking, obtain the tackle and flotation inclusions. For a kayak outing, resolve the rental equipment, fit and launch logistics before buying personal accessories. Wading footwear belongs only on a confirmed wading plan. Use compact storage and shared essentials appropriate to the platform rather than copying an offshore packing list.'),('Keep the bay plan adaptable','Ask about conditions that would change the route or cancel the outing and record an alternative date where possible. The tourism source establishes a range of experiences, not which one is suitable for you on a particular day. This edition uses a single local overview source; exact access and method details still benefit from an additional local check.')],
[kit('Bay target and presentation system','Confirm target, bait or artificial approach and compatible rod/line.','Do not infer the complete setup from the destination name.'),kit('Platform-specific footwear','Select boat, shore or confirmed wading footwear.','Chest waders are not a default for a guided skiff.'),kit('Flotation and rental provision','For a boat or kayak, confirm fitting equipment and inclusions.','Avoid purchasing accessories before knowing the rental package.'),kit('Compact bay-day storage','Use existing storage sized to the actual platform.','Protect personal essentials without duplicating every party member’s kit.','small fishing dry bag','optional')],['Treating kayak, shore and skiff preparation as identical.','Interpreting broad year-round opportunity as a daily forecast.'],['Which platform and departure fit our experience?','What target and presentation suit the actual conditions?','What equipment do you supply or rent?'],access=['Skiff'])
